    "https://www.jobs.gla.ac.uk/job/research-scientist-scottish-marine-animal-stranding-scheme-smass-6028530": {
      "title": "Research Scientist",
      "description": "Research Scientist - Scottish Marine Animal · University of Glasgow Vacancies About Us Contact Why UofG Login Register Login Menu Vacancies About Us Why UofG Contact Sign In Home Jobs Research Scientist - Scottish Marine Animal Stranding Scheme (SMASS) Research Scientist - Scottish Marine Animal Stranding Scheme (SMASS) Back to jobs Featured Research Scientist - Scottish Marine Animal Stranding Scheme (SMASS) Job details Posted 14 September 2026 Salary Grade 6 £33,951 - £37,694 Grade 7 £41,064 - £46,049 Location Glasgow Job Type Research and Teaching Reference 207133 Expiry 12 Oct 2026 23:45 Job description Job Purpose To undertake high-quality research within the University of Glasgow’s Scottish Marine Animal Stranding Scheme (SMASS), one of the longest-running marine mammal disease surveillance systems in the world. For over thirty years SMASS has investigated the health and mortality of whales, dolphins, seals and other marine animals around Scotland’s coast, and the postholder would become an integral part of that work. Working with the Principal Investigator, the postholder will apply specialist scientific knowledge and independent investigation to the capture, structuring, visualisation and analysis of marine mammal strandings and pathology data, making a leading contribution to the scheme’s research outputs, publications, funding applications and collaborative activities. At Grade 7, the postholder will apply advanced specialist expertise to develop research methodologies, demonstrate a growing external research profile, and contribute to research leadership, supervision and wider academic activities. Role Overview The postholder will support two complementary strands of work. The first is operational and analytical: leading the maintenance and continued development of the SMASS strandings database, necropsy portal and sample archives, overseeing the BeachTrack mobile application and its supporting data infrastructure, and producing the analysis, visualisations and outputs required for reporting, outreach and communication. The second is providing technical input to the LIFE CIBBRiNA project, scoping a harmonised, web-accessible database for sharing marine mammal and ETP (Endangered, Threatened and Protected) species strandings and bycatch data, in collaboration with the International Whaling Commission (IWC), ASCOBANS and European partners. Beyond this core remit, the post offers scope to engage with the full breadth of SMASS activities, including strandings coordination and response, post-mortem examination, volunteer support, knowledge exchange and outreach. It will suit someone with a genuine interest in marine mammal pathology and the fieldwork that underpins it, and will include attendance at strandings and post-mortems. Main Duties and Responsibilities Perform the following activities in conjunction with, and under the guidance of, the Principal Investigator: Database management and development 1. Take a leading role in maintaining and developing the centralised relational SMASS database and necropsy portal, including oversight of the BeachTrack data infrastructure; lead and document updates and new features, working with the University of Glasgow Software Services (GUSS) development team to implement them. 2. Develop and embed pathological attributes within the database, applying an understanding of marine mammal pathophysiology to capture necropsy findings for curation, analysis and reporting. 3. Set and uphold data-entry standards, and undertake validation, quality assurance and curation of both new records and the historical strandings archive; harmonise and format data for statutory and international reporting, resolving data inconsistencies and maintaining consistent documentation and metadata throughout. 4. Contribute to the pathological and epidemiological coding and data entry of necropsy reports, and develop improved metrics and methodologies for diagnosing bycatch from pathology data. Data analysis, interpretation and visualisation 5. Undertake statistical, temporal and spatial analysis of strandings data, including trend and time-series analysis. 6. Produce descriptive statistics, tables, figures and distribution maps, together with the analytical and interpretive content underpinning the quarterly and annual reports to government. 7. Develop tools, resources and outputs — including clear data visualisations and basic spatial and mapping (GIS) outputs — that support the exploration, interpretation and communication of the scheme’s scientific, statutory and communication needs. European data harmonisation (technical input and scoping) 8. Provide technical input to the scoping of a harmonised, web-accessible database for collating and assessing marine mammal and ETP species strandings and bycatch data at a European scale, defining the technical requirements to meet the monitoring needs of the relevant case studies. 9. Liaise closely with the IWC, which holds responsibility for delivery of the harmonised database, and advise on the data structures, harmonisation protocols and coding schemes required for consistency across national schemes and marine regions. 10. Coordinate with participating countries and assist regional organisations (such as the IWC, ICES and ASCOBANS) in the transition to, and active use of, a Global Strandings Database. Data requests, external data flows and statutory reporting 11. Handle external data and sample-access requests, providing extracts and summaries to researchers, collaborators, the Marine Directorate and partner organisations in line with institutional and data-protection requirements. 12. Maintain the public data and sample catalogue and the data dashboard, ensuring data integrity, accessibility and currency. 13. Compile, format and submit, in a timely manner, statutory and international data returns, including the data-sharing, reporting and compliance obligations owed to UK CSIP and to international bodies (MSFD, OSPAR, ICES, ASCOBANS and the IWC) under the relevant memoranda of understanding. 14. Support and train SMASS staff and volunteers in data entry and use of the database, communicating data processes and issues clearly to non-technical colleagues. Strandings response, volunteer support and operations 15. Participate in SMASS field strandings response, including the on-call coordination rota, and assist with necropsies, sample processing and archiving, including out-of-hours work in field and beach conditions. 16. Assist the SMASS volunteer training coordinator in delivering training courses for the volunteer network. 17. Provide administrative support to the SMASS programme, including the ordering and management of stock and consumable levels. 18. Develop and maintain digital content and tools to enhance the online presence and accessibility of the SMASS programme. Research, communication and professional development 19. Collaborate with internal and external partners to advance the research aims of SMASS and the broader strategic goals of the University of Glasgow and associated research networks, including contributing to publications, presentations, public engagement and the identification of future funding as opportunities allow. 20. Engage in continuing professional development, keep up to date with advances in the field, and contribute to the enhancement of the University’s international profile in line with its strategic plan. 21. Undertake any other relevant duties, commensurate with the grade and nature of the post, as directed by the Principal Investigator. For appointment at Grade 7, in addition to the above: 22. Take a leading role in developing the underpinning research methodologies that deliver high-quality research deliverables aligned with the scheme’s and the School’s priorities, exercising a higher degree of independence, leadership and responsibility. 23. Take a leading role in translating research outputs and analytical findings into clear concepts, insights and key messages for diverse audiences, maintaining an active portfolio of communication outputs across appropriate digital platforms and engagement channels. 24. Keep abreast of emerging methods, technologies and infrastructure, advising senior colleagues on opportunities and gaps and recommending plans for growth and sustainability. 25. Establish and sustain a track record of independent and joint published research, contribute to grant applications and research proposals, and mentor colleagues and junior researchers. Deliverables 1. Reliable, well-documented delivery and maintenance of the SMASS strandings data and sample archive, with pathological attributes embedded and a suite of data visualisations and reporting outputs in regular use. 2. A scoping report for the European harmonised database, detailing the proposed architecture and data-sharing protocols developed as technical input to the IWC-led delivery. 3. Established and documented harmonised data-sharing protocols to inform the consortium’s adoption of the central database. Knowledge, Qualifications, Skills and Experience Knowledge/Qualifications Essential A1 SCQF level 10 (Honours degree) in a relevant subject or a cognate discipline (e.g. veterinary medicine, biological or marine science, or ecological data management), or an equivalent combination of relevant training and demonstrable practical experience — including, where relevant, a veterinary or medical qualification. A2 Demonstrable understanding of research design appropriate to the discipline, and familiarity with marine animal (or comparable) pathology and post-mortem examination — whether through a veterinary or medical background, formal training, or clear practical experience. Formal veterinary, medical or pathology training is not required, but applicants must be familiar with pathological material and nomenclature. A3 A genuine interest in marine mammal strandings and a willingness to participate in field response and necropsy. A4 Up-to-date knowledge of the legislative requirements applicable to the role, including but not limited to Health and Safety and data protection (GDPR). For appointment at Grade 7, in addition to the above: A5 Postgraduate qualification (SCQF level 11, Master’s degree or equivalent professional experience); or the equivalent competencies acquired through significant relevant experience in strandings-related work in a similar or related role(s). A6 A comprehensive and up-to-date knowledge of current issues and future directions within marine mammal pathology, strandings science or a cognate specialism. Desirable B1 An awarded (or recently submitted / near completion) PhD in a relevant scientific, technical or professional discipline. B2 Familiarity with the conservation status of marine species in the North-East Atlantic and with the technical challenges of bycatch estimation. B3 Working towards, or holding, professional registration with a relevant body (e.g. RCVS for veterinary applicants, or Chartered Scientist (CSci) / Registered Scientist (RSci) / MRSB via an appropriate professional body). Skills Essential C1 Strong attention to detail and accuracy, with a methodical, rigorous approach to recording, validating and structuring data. C2 Sound general IT skills, including proficiency in data-handling tools (e.g. Excel and shared storage systems such as OneDrive) and the aptitude to work with databases. C3 Clear written and verbal communication skills, including the ability to communicate complex information clearly and concisely and to explain data processes and issues to non-technical colleagues. C4 A collaborative, patient and flexible approach to teamworking, including working with University of Glasgow programmers, project teams, researchers and external collaborators. C5 Ability to plan and prioritise a broad range of work, and to manage multiple datasets and tasks simultaneously to meet competing demands. C6 Proficiency in R programming language (or a comparable environment) for data handling, analysis and visualisation, or a strong aptitude and clear willingness to develop it. C7 Self-motivation, initiative and independent thought, with a flexible and pragmatic approach to problem solving. For appointment at Grade 7, in addition to the above: C8 Advanced specialist knowledge within marine mammal pathology, strandings analysis or database harmonisation, including relevant technical models, equipment or techniques. C9 Demonstrable expertise in conceptualising research projects and translating complex findings into clear, accessible outputs for both expert collaborators and non-specialist audiences, including through digital communication platforms. Desirable D1 Experience of data visualisation and of producing clear graphical and reporting outputs (e.g. ggplot2 or comparable tools), and of statistical, temporal or time-series analysis of ecological or comparable data. D2 Familiarity with relational databases and basic SQL, and with data formats and shared storage systems. D3 Basic GIS or spatial-data skills. D4 Experience in developing assessment toolkits for ETP (Endangered, Threatened, or Protected) species and in managing large-scale marine datasets. Experience Essential E1 Relevant work experience within a similar research, laboratory or field environment, including evidence of previous outputs and professional development. E2 Experience of data entry, validation and cleaning processes, and of maintaining documentation and metadata records. E3 Experience of operating independently in progressing and delivering goals within set timescales. E4 Experience of developing tools, resources, software or analytical outputs that support the interpretation, exploration or presentation of research findings. E5 Experience of contributing to public engagement, academic outreach and research publications. For appointment at Grade 7, in addition to the above: E6 Experience of working independently in a similar research environment to recommend and/or determine appropriate research methods, and of establishing and sustaining a track record of independent and joint published research. E7 Experience of supervising students or junior researchers, and of working on complex multi-partner projects. E8 A track record of presentation and publication of research results in quality journals and at conferences. E9 Experience of making a leading contribution in academic activities, and the ability to demonstrate independence — for example through the identification of project objectives from assessment of the literature, the design and analysis of investigations, and the drafting of papers. E10  Experience of undertaking independent research. Desirable F1 Experience of strandings response and/or necropsy, and of the strandings data workflow from the field through to analysis. For appointment at Grade 7, in addition to the above: F2 Experience of understanding and interpreting the technical requirements of service users and implementing appropriate methods of meeting their needs. F3 Experience of managing budgets or stock, including monitoring outgoings on behalf of others. F4 An emerging national or international reputation. For enquiries regarding this post please contact Dr Andrew Brownlow, Andrew.Brownlow@glasgow.ac.uk Terms and Conditions Salary will be Grade 6/7, £33,951 - £37,694 / £41,064 - £46,049 per annum This post is full time, and has funding for up to 15 months. ** The primary place of work for this role will be Glasgow. Flexible working arrangements may be considered for candidates based elsewhere in Scotland, subject to business requirements and the ability to attend and respond to stranding events as required. The University also has a base near Inverness, and consideration may be given to candidates wishing to be based there. However, applicants should be aware that the role will require regular travel to Glasgow. ** The University of Glasgow has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK.  If you require a Skilled Worker visa to work in the UK, you will be required to meet the eligibility requirements of the visa route to be assigned a Certificate of Sponsorship. Please note that this post may be eligible to be sponsored under the Skilled Worker visa route if tradeable points can be used under the Skilled Worker visa rules. For more information please visit: https://www.gov.uk/skilled-worker-visa. As a valued member of our team, you can expect: 1 A warm welcoming and engaging organisational culture, where your talents are developed and nurtured, and success is celebrated and shared. 2 An excellent employment package with generous terms and conditions including 41 days of leave for full time staff, pension - pensions handbook https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/payandpensions/pensions/, benefits and discount packages. 3 A flexible approach to working. 4 A commitment to support your health and wellbeing, including a free 6-month UofG Sport membership for all new staff joining the University  https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/staff/healthwellbeing/. We believe that we can only reach our full potential through the talents of all. Equality, diversity and inclusion are at the heart of our values. Applications are particularly welcome from across our communities and in particular people from the Black, Asian and Minority Ethnic (BAME) community, and other protected characteristics who are under-represented within the University. Read more on how the University promotes and embeds all aspects of equality and diversity within our community https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/humanresources/equalitydiversity/. We endorse the principles of Athena Swan https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/humanresources/equalitydiversity/athenaswan/ and hold bronze, silver and gold awards across the University. We are investing in our organisation, and we will invest in you too. Please visit our website https://www.gla.ac.uk/explore/jobs/ for more information. Closing Date: 12 October 2026 at 23:45 Apply Now Save this job Research Scientist - Scottish Marine Animal Stranding Scheme (SMASS) Glasgow Posted 28 days ago Apply Now Share this job linkedin twitter facebook mail whatsapp Sign up for job alerts Sign in or register here to receive emails about new jobs like these You agree to our Terms & conditions Latest Jobs Research Associate in Evidence Synthesis Glasgow GRADE 7, £41,064-£46,049 per annum People and OD (HR) Systems Coordinator Glasgow Grade 6, £33,951 - £37,694 per annum Research Associate - Applied Research & Knowledge Exchange Glasgow Grade 7:  £41,064 - £46,049 per annum Research Associate - Delivering Humanised Places Glasgow Grade 7:  £41,064 - £46,049 per annum Project Manager Glasgow £41,064 pa - £46,049 pa logo The University of Glasgow is a registered Scottishcharity: Registration Number SC004401 X Facebook instagram Legal Cookies Terms of use PeopleXD accessibilty statement Candidate privacy notice Accreditation Quick Links About Contact Us Vacancies Salaries Applicant Guide All Right Reserved 2026 University of Glasgow Recruitment website by"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.gla.ac.uk/job/clinician-in-small-animal-cardiology-5999138": {
      "title": "Clinician in Small Animal Cardiology · University of Glasgow",
      "description": "Clinician in Small Animal Cardiology · University of Glasgow This website uses cookies to ensure you get the best experience. read more Got it! Vacancies About Us Contact Why UofG Login Register Login Menu Vacancies About Us Why UofG Contact Sign In Home Jobs Clinician in Small Animal Cardiology Clinician in Small Animal Cardiology Back to jobs Featured Clinician in Small Animal Cardiology Job details Posted 18 August 2026 Salary Grade 7/8: £41,064 - £46,049/£50,253 - £58,225 per annum Location Glasgow Job Type Research and Teaching Reference 205733 Expiry 26 Oct 2026 23:45 Job description The University of Glasgow’s School of Biodiversity, One Health and Veterinary Medicine is seeking to recruit a dynamic and motivated clinician to join our Small Animal Cardiology team. We welcome applications from candidates who hold an ECVIM/ACVIM (Cardiology) diploma or have completed an approved residency training programme. In addition to clinical activities, the post offers a significant element of ‘off clinics’ time for research and scholarship. The role will also involve the delivery of clinical teaching to undergraduates. The successful candidate will be joining a friendly and supportive multidisciplinary team within our award-winning state-of-the-art Small Animal Hospital. The hospital is based on the beautiful Garscube estate, 10 minutes’ drive from the vibrant “West End” of Glasgow and less than one hour from Loch Lomond. Our facilities include on-site 1.5T MRI, CT, C-arm fluoroscopy, a range of Olympus and KarlStorz endoscopes, a linear accelerator and radio-iodine unit. We have a dedicated well-equipped ICU and excellent in-house laboratory.  Our ‘One Hospital’ team ethos ensures exceptional clinical care can be delivered to our patients, and supports the continual professional development of our vets, nurses and support staff. The ability to work well with others in a collegial team atmosphere is essential. Job Purpose To deliver an efficient and effective cardiology service by providing expertise and clinical activity in the Small Animal Hospital. To enhance and develop the delivery of clinical teaching to undergraduates, clinical scholars and to undertake clinical research/scholarship within the cardiology service thereby contributing to the School objectives relevant to national and international excellence in clinical service, teaching and research. Main Duties and Responsibilities Clinical: 1. To provide a high-quality cardiology service and appropriate advice to external referring practices and owners that will consequently maintain a clinical referral workload necessary to support the commercial, teaching and research activities. 2. To support the development of novel approaches and techniques to aid diagnosis and treatment and exploit the opportunities to enhance the income generation capability of the service. 3. To contribute to the implementation and validation of clinical quality assurance measures. 4. To participate in the out-of-hours service, ensuring adequate cover is available for the provision of direct supervision, advice and expertise when required. 5. To support the planning, organisation and delivery of training requirements of the clinical scholars, by supervision of clinical duties and research projects, with a view to their successful completion of appropriate Board examinations. 5. To participate in practical/professional and/or clinical practice bodies related to cardiology area at national and international level as appropriate. 6. To ensure compliance with the continuing professional development requirements of the speciality and the RCVS. 7. To participate in promotional activities and events relating to the Small Animal Hospital. For appointment at Grade 8: Teaching: 8. To engage in the delivery of undergraduate and postgraduate teaching activities, as directed by the Head of Division in consultation with the course leaders. 9. To contribute to the development of creative and innovative approaches to teaching and learning, in accordance with the University’s Quality Enhancement Strategy, so that the educational opportunities available to students are maintained or improved. 10. To engage in the delivery of a range of assessment activities and engage fully in the examination process as appropriate, (using a variety of methods and techniques) and provide effective, timely and appropriate feedback to students to support learning. For appointment at Grade 8: Clinical Research/Scholarship: 11. To collaborate in clinical investigations and evidence-based medicine of an external reputation and develop a consistent output of publications in high quality refereed clinical journals and/or textbooks. 12. To attend and participate in national/international events/research seminars/conferences within the service area. For appointment at Grade 8: Administration: 13. To develop material to promote the cardiology service both at conferences and electronically. 14. To undertake divisional/school level administration as requested by the Head of School/Division. To contribute to the performance and development review processes as appropriate in accordance with university strategy. Knowledge, Qualifications, Skills and Experience Knowledge/Qualifications Essential: A1 Veterinary degree registerable with RCVS. A2 Completion of recognised residency training programme. For appointment at Grade 8: A3 ECVIM-CA (Cardiology) or ACVIM Cardiology Diplomate Status. Desirable: B1 PhD (or other evidence of research training). B2 Hold a relevant teaching qualification. B3 Ability to implementing and running your own residency program. Skills Essential: C1 High standard of communication skills both orally and written. C2 Ability to work as part of a team. C3 Competence across the field, corresponding to training and qualifications, and ability to provide expert advice to clinicians, external veterinary surgeons, research bodies and organisations. C4 Self-motivated, able to plan for contingencies and think strategically. C5 Ability to work independently or as part of a team. C6 High standard of presentation skills. For appointment at Grade 8: C7 Expertise across the field, corresponding to training and qualifications, and ability to provide expert advice to clinicians, external veterinary surgeons, research bodies and organisations. C8 Ability to work independently and as part of a team, and to provide leadership. C9 Ability to supervise PGR and residency students. Desirable: D1 Understanding and experience of databases and other IT applications/packages. D2 Leadership of a clinical or teaching team. Experience Essential: E1 Postgrad clinical experience in cardiology speciality. E2 Evidence of clinical publications. For appointment at Grade 8: E3 An established reputation as an advanced clinician in small animal cardiology. Desirable: F1 Teaching or training experience at undergraduate level. F2 Ability up/and or running a specialist clinic. F3 Evidence of peer reviewing scholarly outputs. F4 Teaching experience relevant to the HE sector. F5 A track record of clinical research publications. F6 Record of award of competitive grant funding for clinical research. Note: Candidates having completed a recognised residency training programme and Board-eligible would be appointed at Grade 7 and will be promoted to Grade 8 immediately upon passing the Board exams.  Candidates with suitable relevant experience will be considered for appointment at Grade 7. Terms and Conditions Salary will be Grade 7/8, £41,064 - £46,049/£50,253 - £58,225 per annum, plus an additional generous market and OOH supplement, plus employer pension contribution. This post is full time (35 Hours Per Week) and open ended. The successful candidate for this post will be enrolled onto the University’s Early Career Development Programme (ECDP). This will provide you as an early career academic staff member to be developed and supported over a specified timeframe to facilitate the advancement of your academic career.  Information on the programme can be found here . All applicants must have the right to live and work in the UK or be eligible to obtain permission to work in the UK. This role may be eligible for sponsorship under the Skilled Worker visa route, subject to meeting the relevant Home Office requirements, including any applicable tradeable points criteria. Further information can be found on the University of Glasgow’s UKVI Website . For research and academic roles, we will also consider whether the Global Talent visa route may be available, subject to individual eligibility. As a valued member of our team, you can expect: 1 A warm welcoming and engaging organisational culture, where your talents are developed and nurtured, and success is celebrated and shared. 2 An excellent employment package with generous terms and conditions including 41 days of leave for full time staff, pension , benefits and discount packages. 3 A flexible approach to working. 4 A commitment to support your health and wellbeing , including a free 6-month UofG Sport membership for all new staff joining the University. We believe that we can only reach our full potential through the talents of all. Equality, diversity and inclusion are at the heart of our values. Applications are particularly welcome from across our communities and in particular people from the Black, Asian and Minority Ethnic (BAME) community, and other protected characteristics who are under-represented within the University. Read more on how the University promotes and embeds all aspects of equality and diversity within our community here . We endorse the principles of Athena Swan and hold bronze, silver and gold awards across the University. We are investing in our organisation, and we will invest in you too. Please visit our website https://www.gla.ac.uk/explore/jobs/ for more information. Informal enquiries should be directed to Dr Frane Ivasovic, Frane.Ivasovic@glasgow.ac.uk Closing Date: 26th October 2026 at 23:45. Apply Now Save this job Clinician in Small Animal Cardiology Glasgow Posted about 2 months ago Apply Now Share this job linkedin twitter facebook mail whatsapp Sign up for job alerts Sign in or register here to receive emails about new jobs like these You agree to our Terms & conditions Latest Jobs Research Associate in Evidence Synthesis Glasgow GRADE 7, £41,064-£46,049 per annum People and OD (HR) Systems Coordinator Glasgow Grade 6, £33,951 - £37,694 per annum Research Associate - Applied Research & Knowledge Exchange Glasgow Grade 7:  £41,064 - £46,049 per annum Research Associate - Delivering Humanised Places Glasgow Grade 7:  £41,064 - £46,049 per annum Project Manager Glasgow £41,064 pa - £46,049 pa logo The University of Glasgow is a registered Scottishcharity: Registration Number SC004401 X Facebook instagram Legal Cookies Terms of use PeopleXD accessibilty statement Candidate privacy notice Accreditation Quick Links About Contact Us Vacancies Salaries Applicant Guide All Right Reserved 2026 University of Glasgow Recruitment website by"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.gla.ac.uk/job/teaching-assistant-5957044": {
      "title": "Teaching Assistant",
      "description": "Teaching Assistant · University of Glasgow Vacancies About Us Contact Why UofG Login Register Login Menu Vacancies About Us Why UofG Contact Sign In Home Jobs Teaching Assistant Teaching Assistant Back to jobs Featured Teaching Assistant Job details Posted 06 July 2026 Salary Grade 6: £33,951 - £37,694 per annum pro rata Location Glasgow Job Type Research and Teaching Reference 203396 Expiry 29 Oct 2026 23:45 Job description Job Purpose The postholder will make an important contribution to undergraduate teaching and enhancement of the student learning experience, with particular reference to clinical skills and patient safety. The appointee will play a key role in advancing the Nursing & Health Care School's strategic aim to be an internationally recognised centre of excellence for research and education and will strengthen educational and clinical partnership working, positively contributing to the quality of future care provision. The post offers a development opportunity for those with an emerging interest in the sector to gain experience and develop skills for future career growth. Main Duties and Responsibilities 1. Contribute to a range of teaching, course support and assessment activities at undergraduate level. 2. Liaise with subject experts to develop appropriate teaching materials that meet learning objectives in the subject area. 3. Work with staff with relevant expertise to plan, deliver and evaluate a range of innovative teaching activities and strategies to address clinical skills development and the patient safety agenda in the BN (Hons) programme. 4. Provide effective, timely and appropriate feedback to students to support their learning, using a variety of methods and techniques recommended by staff. 5. Contribute to the supervision of undergraduate and postgraduate student projects as appropriate to the role. 6. Engage in clinical practice and relevant continuing professional development as appropriate to maintain a current knowledge and skill base to ensure application of current, research-informed teaching. 7. Work with others on educational and scholarship initiatives and, where appropriate, in preparing publications to support achievement of excellence in learning and teaching. 8. Contribute to quality assurance and enhancement activities, curriculum review and programme re-accreditation with the University and professional bodies, liaising with others, to ensure high quality education provision is maintained and to take opportunities for external examining. 9. Undertake a pastoral role for students and refer for additional support/guidance as appropriate. 10. Engage in academic professional development as appropriate. 11. Contribute to the enhancement of the University’s international profile in line with the University’s Strategic Plan: University of Glasgow - Explore - Strategy 2025. Knowlegdge, Qualifications, Skills & Experience Knowledge/Qualifications Essential: A1 Honours degree or higher degree. A2 Current adult nursing registration with the NMC. Desirable: B1 Professional teaching qualification. Skills Essential: C1  Excellent communication skills, both oral and written. C2  Excellent interpersonal skills. C3  Time management skills. C4  Ability to work as part of a team. C5  Ability to work with minimal supervision. C6  Self-motivation. C7  Teaching and presentation skills. Experience Essential: E1 Experience of teaching. E2 Relevant current clinical experience. E3 Supervision of students in clinical practice. Terms and Conditions Salary will be Grade 6, £33,951 - £37,694 per annum pro rata. This post is part-time (7 hours per week), and open ended. This role is not eligible for sponsorship under the UK Skilled Worker visa route. Candidates must therefore have an existing right to work in the UK or be able to secure this independently prior to commencing employment. For further information on alternative immigration options, please visit University of Glasgow’s UKVI Website . As a valued member of our team, you can expect: 1 A warm welcoming and engaging organisational culture, where your talents are developed and nurtured, and success is celebrated and shared. 2 An excellent employment package with generous terms and conditions including 41 days of leave for full time staff, pension , benefits and discount packages. 3 A flexible approach to working. 4 A commitment to support your health and wellbeing , including a free 6-month UofG Sport membership for all new staff joining the University. We believe that we can only reach our full potential through the talents of all. Equality, diversity and inclusion are at the heart of our values. Applications are particularly welcome from across our communities and in particular people from the Black, Asian and Minority Ethnic (BAME) community, and other protected characteristics who are under-represented within the University. Read more on how the University promotes and embeds all aspects of equality and diversity within our community here . We endorse the principles of Athena Swan and hold bronze, silver and gold awards across the University. We are investing in our organisation, and we will invest in you too. Please visit our website https://www.gla.ac.uk/explore/jobs/ for more information. Informal enquiries should be directed to Professor Eileen Cowey, Eileen.Cowey@glasgow.ac.uk Closing Date: 29th October 2026 at 23:45. Apply Now Save this job Teaching Assistant Glasgow Posted 3 months ago Apply Now Share this job linkedin twitter facebook mail whatsapp Sign up for job alerts Sign in or register here to receive emails about new jobs like these You agree to our Terms & conditions Latest Jobs Research Associate in Evidence Synthesis Glasgow GRADE 7, £41,064-£46,049 per annum People and OD (HR) Systems Coordinator Glasgow Grade 6, £33,951 - £37,694 per annum Research Associate - Applied Research & Knowledge Exchange Glasgow Grade 7:  £41,064 - £46,049 per annum Research Associate - Delivering Humanised Places Glasgow Grade 7:  £41,064 - £46,049 per annum Project Manager Glasgow £41,064 pa - £46,049 pa logo The University of Glasgow is a registered Scottishcharity: Registration Number SC004401 X Facebook instagram Legal Cookies Terms of use PeopleXD accessibilty statement Candidate privacy notice Accreditation Quick Links About Contact Us Vacancies Salaries Applicant Guide All Right Reserved 2026 University of Glasgow Recruitment website by"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.gla.ac.uk/job/research-associate-in-evidence-synthesis-6057685": {
      "title": "Research Associate in Evidence Synthesis",
      "description": "Research Associate in Evidence Synthesis · University of Glasgow Vacancies About Us Contact Why UofG Login Register Login Menu Vacancies About Us Why UofG Contact Sign In Home Jobs Research Associate in Evidence Synthesis Research Associate in Evidence Synthesis Back to jobs Research Associate in Evidence Synthesis Job details Posted 12 October 2026 Salary GRADE 7, £41,064-£46,049 per annum Location Glasgow Job Type Research and Teaching Reference 210233 Expiry 26 Oct 2026 23:45 Job description Job Purpose To make a leading contribution to evidence synthesis research within the Evidence Synthesis Group at Complex Reviews Synthesis Unit (CRSU).  The Evidence Synthesis Group @CRSU, embedded within HEHTA, is funded by the National Institute for Health and Care Research (NIHR).  The aim of the Group is to address knowledge gaps, and to answer specific needs of clinical and health policy decision-makers across the four nations of the UK. The successful candidate will also have opportunities to contribute to evidence synthesis activities within HEHTA.  They will lead the formulation and submission of research publications and research proposals as well as help manage and direct complex and challenging projects as opportunities allow. Main Duties and Responsibilities 1.Play a leading role in the management and development of evidence synthesis projects, and to work independently and in collaboration to bring the project to a successful completion.  This will include establishing and engaging with external collaborators and stakeholders. 2.Play a leading role in the planning and conduct of assigned research individually or jointly in accordance with the project deliverables and Evidence Synthesis Group/HEHTA research strategy. 3.Establish and maintain an extensive research profile and reputation and that of the University/HEHTA.  This include sustaining a track record of independent and joint publications of high-quality publications, enhancing the research impact in terms of economic/societal benefit, and gathering indicators of esteem. 4.Presentation of research to policy decision-makers, at international and national conferences, at internal and external seminars, colloquia and workshops to develop and enhance the research profile of HEHTA/the University. 5. Lead the identification of potential funding sources and the development of proposals to secure funding from internal and external bodies to support future research. 6. Take a leading role in developing and maintaining collaborations with colleagues across HEHTA/the University and wider community. 7. Play a leading role in the organisation, supervision, mentoring and training of undergraduate and/or postgraduate students and less experienced members of the project team to ensure their effective development. 8. Supervise postgraduate taught and postgraduate research students as assigned by Director of HEHTA. 9. Keep up to date with current knowledge and recent advances in the field/discipline.  Engage in personal, professional and career development, to enhance both specialist and transferable skills in accordance with desired career trajectory. 10.Engage in personal, professional and career development, to enhance both specialist and transferable skills in accordance with desired career trajectory. 11.Undertake any other reasonable duties as required by the Director of HEHTA. Contribute to the enhancement of the University’s international profile in line with the University Strategy. Knowledge, Qualifications, Skills & Experience Knowledge, Qualifications Essential A1. Normally Scottish Credit and Qualification Framework level 12 (PhD) plus track record of emerging independence within a research/professional environment, or alternatively possess professional qualifications and experience equivalent to PhD level plus the requisite experience. A2 A comprehensive and up-to-date knowledge of current issues and future directions within the subject area of evidence synthesis A3 Knowledge of translating complex clinical and health questions into appropriate research questions for evidence synthesis A4 Knowledge of advanced evidence synthesis methodologies Desirable B1 Knowledge of health economics and health technology assessment Skills Essential C1 Excellent skills in appropriate statistical software commonly used in evidence synthesis (e.g. R, Stata, WinBUGS) C2 Excellent communication skills (oral and written), including the ability to communicate complex/specialist concepts clearly and concisely to a broad range of stakeholders C3 Proven ability to deliver quality outputs in a timely and efficient manner C4 A systematic and thorough approach to work with high level of attention to detail C5 A commitment to team working, professionalism and respect for others Experience Essential E1 Proven track record in undertaking evidence synthesis (including systematic reviews, overviews, scoping reviews) of various types of evidence, independently or under minimal supervision Desirable F1 Experience in delivering teaching in evidence synthesis F2 A strong track record of publishing evidence synthesis in peer-reviewed journals F3 Sufficient depth of relevant research experience, normally including sufficient postdoctoral experience in a related field, appropriate to an early career researcher. Informal Enquiries should be directed to Professor Olivia Wu, Olivia.Wu@glasgow.ac.uk Terms and Conditions Salary will be Grade 7, £41,064 - £46,049 per annum. This post is full time, and has funding up to 31 March 2028. All applicants must have the right to live and work in the UK or be eligible to obtain permission to work in the UK. This role may be eligible for sponsorship under the Skilled Worker visa route, subject to meeting the relevant Home Office requirements, including any applicable tradeable points criteria. Further information can be found on the University of Glasgow’s UKVI Website. For research and academic roles, we will also consider whether the Global Talent visa route may be available, subject to individual eligibility. As a valued member of our team, you can expect: 1 A warm welcoming and engaging organisational culture, where your talents are developed and nurtured, and success is celebrated and shared. 2 An excellent employment package with generous terms and conditions including 41 days of leave for full time staff, pension - pensions handbook https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/payandpensions/pensions/, benefits and discount packages. 3 A flexible approach to working. 4 A commitment to support your health and wellbeing, including a free 6-month UofG Sport membership for all new staff joining the University  https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/staff/healthwellbeing/. We believe that we can only reach our full potential through the talents of all. Equality, diversity and inclusion are at the heart of our values. Applications are particularly welcome from across our communities and in particular people from the Black, Asian and Minority Ethnic (BAME) community, and other protected characteristics who are under-represented within the University. Read more on how the University promotes and embeds all aspects of equality and diversity within our community https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/humanresources/equalitydiversity/. We endorse the principles of Athena Swan https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/humanresources/equalitydiversity/athenaswan/ and hold bronze, silver and gold awards across the University. We are investing in our organisation, and we will invest in you too. Please visit our website https://www.gla.ac.uk/explore/jobs/ for more information. Closing date : 26 October 2026 @23:45 Apply Now Save this job Research Associate in Evidence Synthesis Glasgow Posted about 8 hours ago Apply Now Share this job linkedin twitter facebook mail whatsapp Sign up for job alerts Sign in or register here to receive emails about new jobs like these You agree to our Terms & conditions Latest Jobs Research Associate in Evidence Synthesis Glasgow GRADE 7, £41,064-£46,049 per annum People and OD (HR) Systems Coordinator Glasgow Grade 6, £33,951 - £37,694 per annum Research Associate - Applied Research & Knowledge Exchange Glasgow Grade 7:  £41,064 - £46,049 per annum Research Associate - Delivering Humanised Places Glasgow Grade 7:  £41,064 - £46,049 per annum Project Manager Glasgow £41,064 pa - £46,049 pa logo The University of Glasgow is a registered Scottishcharity: Registration Number SC004401 X Facebook instagram Legal Cookies Terms of use PeopleXD accessibilty statement Candidate privacy notice Accreditation Quick Links About Contact Us Vacancies Salaries Applicant Guide All Right Reserved 2026 University of Glasgow Recruitment website by"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.gla.ac.uk/job/people-and-od-hr-systems-coordinator-6056629": {
      "title": "People and OD (HR) Systems Coordinator",
      "description": "People and OD (HR) Systems Coordinator · University of Glasgow Vacancies About Us Contact Why UofG Login Register Login Menu Vacancies About Us Why UofG Contact Sign In Home Jobs People and OD (HR) Systems Coordinator People and OD (HR) Systems Coordinator Back to jobs People and OD (HR) Systems Coordinator Job details Posted 09 October 2026 Salary Grade 6, £33,951 - £37,694 per annum Location Glasgow Job Type Management Professional & Administrative Reference 210353 Expiry 23 Oct 2026 23:45 Job description Job Purpose System Co-ordinator for the People and OD (HR) system, working in conjunction with the People and OD Systems Manager, ensuring the integrity of the database structure and data in the system and playing a key role in its ongoing development, configuration and roll out of functionality to users. Main Duties and Responsibilities 1. Responsibility for creation and maintenance of central system procedures and access profiles ensuring that P&OD processes are well understood and support the P&OD and people management activities across the University, including data integrity. 2. Undertake the role of system co-ordinator (super user). Oversee and undertake the setup, configuration and maintenance of reference data, terms and condition templates, user security, workflows, user defined fields/screens, standard letter templates thereby ensuring the ongoing integrity of the organisational and authorisation hierarchies. 3. Work with the P&OD Systems Manager, System Supplier and Internal IT to maintain robust change control processes and documentation, ensuring that all system developments and upgrades are effectively managed, tested and implemented. 4. Coordinate implementation of system updates with System Supplier and Internal IT including configuration, testing and roll out to the production environment. Ensure effective consultation and communication regarding changes to business processes is undertaken with appropriate stakeholders. 5. Responsible for capturing and specifying requirements for new areas of functionality, liaising with the software supplier to scope developments and coordinate the testing and implementation of new functionality. 6. Manage and plan the provision of a high-quality customer focussed help desk service, ensuring timely and effective resolutions to system user enquiries and delivery of necessary systems training. 7. Co-ordination of project work to develop/upgrade the system working with IT, HR supplier and other dedicated project resources to enhance effectiveness and efficiency. 8. Consult P&OD Stakeholders to develop new standard reports in line with their requirements. 9. Contribute to training needs analysis of ongoing training requirements for new and existing staff continuously developing and improving the skills of managers and staff using the people system. Plan a schedule of required training and oversee the creation/maintenance of training guides and user manuals/ system documentation, e-learning where appropriate and FAQ’s. 10. Deputise for the P&OD Systems Manager/support the Data and Insights Partner where required and influence the future roadmap of the people system through participation at User groups and liaison with other Core System Users within the UK and Ireland. Knowledge, Qualifications, Skills and Experience Knowledge/Qualifications Essential A1 SVQ 4/HND (SCQF level 8 or equivalent) and experience of personal development in a similar role or the ability to demonstrate the competencies required to undertake the duties associated with this level of post having acquired the necessary knowledge and skills through personal development and progression in a similar related role(s). A2 Comprehensive specialised knowledge of integrated people systems and management reporting software. A3 Well developed understanding of data security and governance A4 High levels of numeracy and analytical capability A5 In-depth knowledge of Microsoft Office including Excel. A6 Thorough conceptual understanding of relational databases. A7 Knowledge of implementing manager and self-service packages including workflow. A8 Understanding of P&OD Organisational Data and related confidentiality issues. Desirable B1 Comprehensive knowledge of the Access Group people system (People XD / CoreHR). B2 Clear understanding of relevant University policies and procedures. B3 Knowledge of Ivanti or other help desk packages B4 Detailed knowledge and understanding of people processes, policies, terminology and procedures. Skills Essential C1 Proven analytical and problem-solving ability C2 Excellent communications skills – both oral and written and the ability to clearly articulate complex issues. C3 Ability to construct accessible and informative data views that integrate data from different databases to provide consolidated management information C4 Ability to present data in an easily digestible format with dashboard functionality eg powerbi C5 Excellent IT skills C6 Advanced abilities in the use of management reporting query tools. C7 Excellent organisational and planning skills. C8 Ability to manage and prioritise workload, meet tight deadlines and work under pressure C9 Well developed people team leading and motivational skills. C10 Strong service delivery ethos C11 Accuracy, numeracy, excellent attention to detail and ability to handle large volumes of data C12 Ability to use initiative, work with minimal supervision and work as part of a team and also across other teams. C13 Ability to maintain confidentiality and deal sensitively with related issues/ individuals. Experience Essential E1 Experience of working in a database team increasing knowledge and experience E2 Experience of creating and analysing management reports for significant staff numbers. E3 Significant experience of relational P&OD databases. E4 Experience of implementing elements of employee and manager self-service processes/tools relating to P&OD. E5 Project co-ordination / management experience. Terms and Conditions Salary will be Grade 6, £33,951 -  £37,694 per annum. This post is full time and fixed term for up to 18 months. The University of Glasgow has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK.  If you require a Skilled Worker visa to work in the UK, you will be required to meet the eligibility requirements of the visa route to be assigned a Certificate of Sponsorship. Please note that this post may be eligible to be sponsored under the Skilled Worker visa route if tradeable points can be used under the Skilled Worker visa rules. For more information please visit: https://www.gov.uk/skilled-worker-visa As a valued member of our team, you can expect: 1 A warm welcoming and engaging organisational culture, where your talents are developed and nurtured, and success is celebrated and shared. 2 An excellent employment package with generous terms and conditions including 41 days of leave for full time staff, pension - pensions handbook https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/payandpensions/pensions/, benefits and discount packages. 3 A flexible approach to working. 4 A commitment to support your health and wellbeing, including a free 6-month UofG Sport membership for all new staff joining the University  https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/staff/healthwellbeing/. We believe that we can only reach our full potential through the talents of all. Equality, diversity and inclusion are at the heart of our values. Applications are particularly welcome from across our communities and in particular people from the Black, Asian and Minority Ethnic (BAME) community, and other protected characteristics who are under-represented within the University. Read more on how the University promotes and embeds all aspects of equality and diversity within our community https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/humanresources/equalitydiversity/. We endorse the principles of Athena Swan https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/humanresources/equalitydiversity/athenaswan/ and hold bronze, silver and gold awards across the University. We are investing in our organisation, and we will invest in you too. Please visit our website https://www.gla.ac.uk/explore/jobs/ for more information. Closing date : 23 October 2026 @23:45 Apply Now Save this job People and OD (HR) Systems Coordinator Glasgow Posted 3 days ago Apply Now Share this job linkedin twitter facebook mail whatsapp Sign up for job alerts Sign in or register here to receive emails about new jobs like these You agree to our Terms & conditions Latest Jobs Research Associate in Evidence Synthesis Glasgow GRADE 7, £41,064-£46,049 per annum People and OD (HR) Systems Coordinator Glasgow Grade 6, £33,951 - £37,694 per annum Research Associate - Applied Research & Knowledge Exchange Glasgow Grade 7:  £41,064 - £46,049 per annum Research Associate - Delivering Humanised Places Glasgow Grade 7:  £41,064 - £46,049 per annum Project Manager Glasgow £41,064 pa - £46,049 pa logo The University of Glasgow is a registered Scottishcharity: Registration Number SC004401 X Facebook instagram Legal Cookies Terms of use PeopleXD accessibilty statement Candidate privacy notice Accreditation Quick Links About Contact Us Vacancies Salaries Applicant Guide All Right Reserved 2026 University of Glasgow Recruitment website by"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.gla.ac.uk/job/research-associate-applied-research-and-knowledge-exchange-6055806": {
      "title": "Research Associate",
      "description": "Research Associate - Applied Research & · University of Glasgow Vacancies About Us Contact Why UofG Login Register Login Menu Vacancies About Us Why UofG Contact Sign In Home Jobs Research Associate - Applied Research & Knowledge Exchange Research Associate - Applied Research & Knowledge Exchange Back to jobs Research Associate - Applied Research & Knowledge Exchange Job details Posted 09 October 2026 Salary Grade 7:  £41,064 - £46,049 per annum End date 28 October 2026 Location Glasgow Job Type Research and Teaching Reference 210155 Expiry 28 Oct 2026 23:45 Job description Job Purpose To develop applied research, knowledge exchange and impact in the field of architecture, placemaking and the built environment. The role is a crucial aspect of the ‘Delivering Humanised Places’ project funded by UKRI and working in partnership with Heatherwick Studio/Humanise. The role requires expert knowledge and demonstrated experience in architecture, placemaking and the built environment. Particular expertise includes applied research across architecture, placemaking and the built environment, including working closely with non-academic partners in the design, delivery, and evaluation of these activities and across disciplinary areas, and experience/understanding of the evolving research and innovation environment. The successful candidate will also be expected to contribute to the formulation and submission of research/KE publications and research proposals as well as help manage and direct this project as opportunities allow. For further details, please contact Professor Rebecca Madgin: rebecca.madgin@glasgow.ac.uk Main Duties and Responsibilities Perform the following activities in conjunction with Professor Madgin and other team members as appropriate: 1.  Survey the research literature and environment, understand the research challenges associated with the project & subject area, and develop/implement a suitable applied research/KE strategy to deliver project/area requirements and opportunities. 2.  Presentation of work at international and national conferences, at internal and external seminars, colloquia and workshops to develop and enhance our research and/or impact profile. 3.  Take a leading role in the identification of potential funding sources and to assist in the development of proposals to secure funding from internal and external bodies to support future applied research and impact. 4.  Take a leading role in developing and maintaining collaborations with colleagues across the research group/area/School/College/University and wider community (e.g. academic and industrial partners). 5.  Take a leading role in team/group meetings/seminars/workshops and School research group/area activities to enhance the wider knowledge, outputs and culture of the School/College. 6.  Take the lead in the organisation, supervision, mentoring and training of undergraduate and/or postgraduate students and less experienced members of the project team to ensure their effective development. 7.  Perform administrative tasks related to the activities of the research group and School, including budgets/expenditure. 8.  Contribute as appropriate to teaching activities (e.g. demonstrating, etc) and associated administration, as may be assigned through the School in consultation with your line manager. 9.  Keep up to date with current knowledge and recent advances in the field/discipline. 10.  Engage in personal, professional and career development, to enhance both specialist and transferable skills in accordance with desired career trajectory. 11.  Undertake any other reasonable duties as required by your line manager and the Head of School. 12.  Contribute to the enhancement of the University’s international profile in line with University strategy. Knowledge, Qualifications, Skills & Experience Knowledge & Qualifications Essential A1 Normally Scottish Credit and Qualification Framework level 12 (PhD) plus track record of emerging independence within a research/professional environment, or alternatively possess professional qualifications and experience equivalent to PhD level plus the requisite experience across architecture, placemaking, and the built environment. A2 A comprehensive and up-to-date knowledge of current issues and future directions within architecture, placemaking, and the built environment. A3 Well-developed working knowledge of applied research methods, technical models, equipment and techniques relevant to architecture, placemaking, and the built environment. Skills Essential C1 Ability to communicate material of a specialist or highly technical nature and to be effective both orally and in writing at a level appropriate to the activity. C2 Developing track record of applied research outputs compatible with enhancing the School’s research profile in accordance with School objectives. C3 Proven commitment to supporting the career development of colleagues and to other forms of collegiality appropriate to the career stage, including some experience of research project supervision. C4 Effective organisation skills to manage time, prioritise and deliver appropriate research, teaching and administrative responsibilities. C5 Commitment to open research, as appropriate to the discipline, through open data, open code, open educational resources and practices that support replication. Experience Essential E1 Experience of planning and progressing work activities within professional guidelines or organisational policy, applying initiative and independent judgement. E2 Experience of interdisciplinary working and understanding of relevant areas within and outside of arts and humanities e.g. architecture, design, placemaking, neuroarchitecture and/or environmental psychology. E3 Experience of making distinct contributions to identifying and pursuing research funding (or equivalent business development) applications. E4 Experience of planning and progressing work activities within professional guidelines or organisational policy, applying initiative and independent judgement. E5 Demonstrated experience of working with external partners to effect policy and practice change and evidence of personal contribution to this. Desirable F1 Experience of participating in activities that translate research into impact to benefit the economy and wider society. F2 Experience of contributing to the design and delivery of teaching and assessment in higher education or other relevant environments. F3 Experience of working internationally with key partners across architecture, placemaking, and the built environment. F4 Awareness of evolving research and innovation as related to architecture, placemaking, and the built environment. Terms and Conditions Salary will be Grade 7, £41,064 - £46,049 per annum. This post is full time (35 hours per week) and fixed term until 31 March 2028 in the first instance. All applicants must have the right to live and work in the UK or be eligible to obtain permission to work in the UK. This role may be eligible for sponsorship under the Skilled Worker visa route, subject to meeting the relevant Home Office requirements, including any applicable tradeable points criteria. Further information can be found on the University of Glasgow’s UKVI Website . For research and academic roles, we will also consider whether the Global Talent visa route may be available, subject to individual eligibility. As a valued member of our team, you can expect: 1 A warm welcoming and engaging organisational culture, where your talents are developed and nurtured, and success is celebrated and shared. 2 An excellent employment package with generous terms and conditions including 41 days of leave for full time staff, pension - pensions handbook https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/payandpensions/pensions/ , benefits and discount packages. 3 A flexible approach to working. 4 A commitment to support your health and wellbeing, including a free 6-month UofG Sport membership for all new staff joining the University https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/staff/healthwellbeing/ . We believe that we can only reach our full potential through the talents of all. Equality, diversity and inclusion are at the heart of our values. Applications are particularly welcome from across our communities and in particular people from the Black, Asian and Minority Ethnic (BAME) community, and other protected characteristics who are under-represented within the University. Read more on how the University promotes and embeds all aspects of equality and diversity within our community https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/humanresources/equalitydiversity/ . We endorse the principles of Athena Swan https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/humanresources/equalitydiversity/athenaswan/ and hold bronze, silver and gold awards across the University. We are investing in our organisation, and we will invest in you too. Please visit our website https://www.gla.ac.uk/explore/jobs/ for more information. Closing Date: 28 October 2026 at 23:45pm Apply Now Save this job Research Associate - Applied Research & Knowledge Exchange Glasgow Posted 3 days ago Apply Now Share this job linkedin twitter facebook mail whatsapp Sign up for job alerts Sign in or register here to receive emails about new jobs like these You agree to our Terms & conditions Latest Jobs Research Associate in Evidence Synthesis Glasgow GRADE 7, £41,064-£46,049 per annum People and OD (HR) Systems Coordinator Glasgow Grade 6, £33,951 - £37,694 per annum Research Associate - Applied Research & Knowledge Exchange Glasgow Grade 7:  £41,064 - £46,049 per annum Research Associate - Delivering Humanised Places Glasgow Grade 7:  £41,064 - £46,049 per annum Project Manager Glasgow £41,064 pa - £46,049 pa logo The University of Glasgow is a registered Scottishcharity: Registration Number SC004401 X Facebook instagram Legal Cookies Terms of use PeopleXD accessibilty statement Candidate privacy notice Accreditation Quick Links About Contact Us Vacancies Salaries Applicant Guide All Right Reserved 2026 University of Glasgow Recruitment website by"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.gla.ac.uk/job/research-associate-delivering-humanised-places-6055259": {
      "title": "Research Associate",
      "description": "Research Associate - Delivering Humanised · University of Glasgow Vacancies About Us Contact Why UofG Login Register Login Menu Vacancies About Us Why UofG Contact Sign In Home Jobs Research Associate - Delivering Humanised Places Research Associate - Delivering Humanised Places Back to jobs Research Associate - Delivering Humanised Places Job details Posted 08 October 2026 Salary Grade 7:  £41,064 - £46,049 per annum End date 28 October 2026 Location Glasgow Job Type Research and Teaching Reference 210154 Expiry 28 Oct 2026 23:45 Job description Job Purpose To develop research, knowledge exchange and impact activities in the field of architecture, design and placemaking. The role is a crucial aspect of the ‘Delivering Humanised Places’ project funded by UKRI and working in partnership with Heatherwick Studio/Humanise. The role requires expert knowledge and demonstrated experience in architecture, placemaking, and the built environment. Particular expertise includes a theoretical/conceptual knowledge of people-centred placemaking, innovative methodological approaches, experience of working across disciplines, experience/willingness to conduct applied research, working with non-academic partners, and a detailed awareness of various place-based policies and practices across the United Kingdom and internationally. The successful candidate will also be expected to contribute to the formulation and submission of research publications and research proposals as well as help manage and direct this project as opportunities allow. For further details, please contact Professor Rebecca Madgin: rebecca.madgin@glasgow.ac.uk Main Duties and Responsibilities Perform the following activities in conjunction with Professor Madgin and other team members as appropriate: 1.  Survey the research literature and environment, understand the research challenges associated with the project & subject area, and develop/implement a suitable research strategy to deliver project/area requirements and opportunities. 2.  Presentation of work at international and national conferences, at internal and external seminars, colloquia and workshops to develop and enhance our research and/or impact profile. 3.  Take a leading role in the identification of potential funding sources and to assist in the development of proposals to secure funding from internal and external bodies to support future research and impact. 4.  Take a leading role in developing and maintaining collaborations with colleagues across the research group/area/School/College/University and wider community (e.g. academic and industrial partners). 5.  Take a leading role in team/group meetings/seminars/workshops and School research group/area activities to enhance the wider knowledge, outputs and culture of the School/College. 6.  Take the lead in the organisation, supervision, mentoring and training of undergraduate and/or postgraduate students and less experienced members of the project team to ensure their effective development. 7.  Perform administrative tasks related to the activities of the research group and School, including budgets/expenditure. 8.  Contribute as appropriate to teaching activities (e.g. demonstrating, etc) and associated administration, as may be assigned through the School in consultation with your line manager. 9.  Keep up to date with current knowledge and recent advances in the field/discipline. 10.  Engage in personal, professional and career development, to enhance both specialist and transferable skills in accordance with desired career trajectory. 11.  Undertake any other reasonable duties as required by your line manager and the Head of School. 12.  Contribute to the enhancement of the University’s international profile in line with University strategy. Knowledge, Qualifications, Skills and Experience Knowledge & Qualifications Essential A1 Normally Scottish Credit and Qualification Framework level 12 (PhD) plus track record of emerging independence within a research/professional environment, or alternatively possess professional qualifications and experience equivalent to PhD level plus the requisite experience across architecture, placemaking, and the built environment. A2 A comprehensive and up-to-date knowledge of current issues and future directions within architecture, placemaking, and the built environment. A3 Well-developed working knowledge of research methods, technical models, equipment and techniques relevant to architecture, placemaking, and the built environment including systematic/scoping reviews. Skills Essential C1 Ability to communicate material of a specialist or highly technical nature and to be effective both orally and in writing at a level appropriate to the activity. C2 Developing track record of research outputs compatible with enhancing the School’s research profile in accordance with School objectives. C3 Proven commitment to supporting the career development of colleagues and to other forms of collegiality appropriate to the career stage, including some experience of research project supervision. C4 Effective organisation skills to manage time, prioritise and deliver appropriate research, teaching and administrative responsibilities. C5 Commitment to open research, as appropriate to the discipline, through open data, open code, open educational resources and practices that support replication. C6 Demonstrated experience of working with external partners to effect policy and practice change and evidence of personal contribution to this. Experience Essential E1 Experience of making distinct contributions to identifying and pursuing research funding (or equivalent business development) applications. E2 Experience of planning and progressing work activities within professional guidelines or organisational policy, applying initiative and independent judgement. E3 Experience of planning and progressing work activities within professional guidelines or organisational policy, applying initiative and independent judgement. E4 Experience of interdisciplinary working and understanding of relevant areas within and outside of arts and humanities e.g. architecture, design, placemaking, neuroarchitecture and/or environmental psychology. Desirable F1 Experience of participating in activities that translate research into impact to benefit the economy and wider society. F2 Experience of contributing to the design and delivery of teaching and assessment in higher education or other relevant environments. F3 Experience of working internationally with key partners across architecture, placemaking, and the built environment. F4 Awareness of evolving research and innovation as related to architecture, placemaking, and the built environment. F5 Evidence of conducting systematic/rapid scoping reviews related to architecture, placemaking, and the built environment. Terms and Conditions Salary will be Grade 7, £41,064 - £46,049 per annum. This post is full time (35 hours per week) and fixed term for up to 15 months in the first instance. All applicants must have the right to live and work in the UK or be eligible to obtain permission to work in the UK. This role may be eligible for sponsorship under the Skilled Worker visa route, subject to meeting the relevant Home Office requirements, including any applicable tradeable points criteria. Further information can be found on the University of Glasgow’s UKVI Website . For research and academic roles, we will also consider whether the Global Talent visa route may be available, subject to individual eligibility As a valued member of our team, you can expect: 1 A warm welcoming and engaging organisational culture, where your talents are developed and nurtured, and success is celebrated and shared. 2 An excellent employment package with generous terms and conditions including 41 days of leave for full time staff, pension - pensions handbook https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/payandpensions/pensions/ , benefits and discount packages. 3 A flexible approach to working. 4 A commitment to support your health and wellbeing, including a free 6-month UofG Sport membership for all new staff joining the University https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/staff/healthwellbeing/ . We believe that we can only reach our full potential through the talents of all. Equality, diversity and inclusion are at the heart of our values. Applications are particularly welcome from across our communities and in particular people from the Black, Asian and Minority Ethnic (BAME) community, and other protected characteristics who are under-represented within the University. Read more on how the University promotes and embeds all aspects of equality and diversity within our community https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/humanresources/equalitydiversity/ . We endorse the principles of Athena Swan https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/humanresources/equalitydiversity/athenaswan/ and hold bronze, silver and gold awards across the University. We are investing in our organisation, and we will invest in you too. Please visit our website https://www.gla.ac.uk/explore/jobs/ for more information. Closing Date:  28 October 2026 at 23:45pm Apply Now Save this job Research Associate - Delivering Humanised Places Glasgow Posted 4 days ago Apply Now Share this job linkedin twitter facebook mail whatsapp Sign up for job alerts Sign in or register here to receive emails about new jobs like these You agree to our Terms & conditions Latest Jobs Research Associate in Evidence Synthesis Glasgow GRADE 7, £41,064-£46,049 per annum People and OD (HR) Systems Coordinator Glasgow Grade 6, £33,951 - £37,694 per annum Research Associate - Applied Research & Knowledge Exchange Glasgow Grade 7:  £41,064 - £46,049 per annum Research Associate - Delivering Humanised Places Glasgow Grade 7:  £41,064 - £46,049 per annum Project Manager Glasgow £41,064 pa - £46,049 pa logo The University of Glasgow is a registered Scottishcharity: Registration Number SC004401 X Facebook 