    "https://www.jobs.gla.ac.uk/job/student-success-assistant-5992821": {
      "title": "Student Success Assistant",
      "description": "Student Success Assistant · University of Glasgow Vacancies About Us Contact Why UofG Login Register Login Menu Vacancies About Us Why UofG Contact Sign In Home Jobs Student Success Assistant Student Success Assistant Back to jobs Student Success Assistant Job details Posted 11 August 2026 Salary Grade 5, £28,031 - £31,236 per annum End date 25 August 2026 Location Glasgow Reference 201499 Expiry 14 Oct 2026 23:45 Job description Please note this vacancy is only open to current contracted University of Glasgow employees.  Candidates who have applied within the last 6 months will not be considered and should not reapply. Job Purpose The Student Success Assistant provides frontline support to students, helping them navigate academic and personal challenges and access the most appropriate sources of help. Working as part of the Student Success team within the wider Student Wellbeing provision, the postholder contributes to early identification of students who may require additional support, conducts initial wellbeing triage, and ensures timely referral or signposting. The role supports the delivery of proactive engagement activity across assigned Schools or units, promotes awareness of available support pathways, and contributes insights that help inform service development. Operating within defined boundaries, the postholder manages a busy and varied workload, maintains accurate records, and collaborates closely with colleagues across academic and professional services to enhance student success, retention, and overall wellbeing. Main Duties and Responsibilities 1. Provide empathetic, accurate, and timely information and advice on academic and personal matters, guiding students to the appropriate academic, wellbeing, and administrative support services. 2. Conduct initial wellbeing triage and refer students to appropriate internal or external services best placed to assist them. 3. Maintain accurate and up-to-date case records, in line with service protocols, to ensure continuity of support and appropriate follow-up. 4. Promote awareness of available support services through proactive engagement with students and assist other teams in sharing clear, accessible information about the support pathways on offer. 5. Support students in navigating university processes and policies including the submission of Extenuating Circumstances, requests for Interruption of Studies, connecting students to academic advising or local learning and teaching teams. And any other relevant advice or support for their needs. 6. Contribute to early intervention strategies for at-risk students by sharing observations and concerns with relevant services or management to ensure at-risk students receive timely support. 7. Work collaboratively with colleagues to gather student feedback, highlight emerging themes, and contribute insights that inform recommendations for service improvements. 8. Maintain strict confidentiality and adhere to data protection requirements, ensuring all student information is managed appropriately and securely. Knowledge, Qualifications, Skills and Experience Knowledge/Qualifications Essential: A1 Ability to demonstrate the competencies required to undertake the duties associated with this level of post having acquired the necessary knowledge and skills in a similar role Or: Scottish Credit and Qualification Framework level 7 (Higher / Scottish Vocational Qualification level 3, Higher National Certificate) or equivalent, and experience of personal development in a similar role. A2 Awareness of higher education processes, support structures and student-facing services.  A3 Understanding of student needs within a higher education environment. Skills Essential: C1 Strong communication and interpersonal skills, with the ability to explain processes and policies clearly to diverse student groups. C2 Ability to provide empathetic, non-judgemental guidance on sensitive academic or personal issues.  C3 Excellent time management, organisational and administrative skills, including accurate record-keeping and the ability to manage competing priorities. C4 Proven ability to conduct initial wellbeing triage, recognise indicators of distress, and escalate appropriately.  C5 Effective IT skills and the ability to work with case management systems. C6 Ability to maintain confidentiality and apply data protection and safeguarding principles professionally.  C7 Skilled at maintaining composure, professionalism, and appropriate boundaries when supporting students in distress. C8 Ability to work as part of an interdisciplinary team? C9 Ability to solve problems as part of a team and resolve issues, plan solutions and make pragmatic decisions. Experience Essential: E1 Experience of delivering frontline or customer facing support in a busy environment. E2 Experience of working under pressure and managing high-volume caseloads.  E3 Experience using sound judgement to identify when a concern should be escalated or when to signpost to specialist support. E4 Experience building effective working relationships with students and colleagues. Terms and Conditions Salary will be Grade 5, £28,031 - £31,236 per annum. This post is full time and open ended. Closing date: 23:45 on 14th October 2026 As a valued member of our team, you can expect: 1 A warm welcoming and engaging organisational culture, where your talents are developed and nurtured, and success is celebrated and shared. 2 An excellent employment package with generous terms and conditions including 41 days of leave for full time staff, pension - pensions handbook https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/payandpensions/pensions/, benefits and discount packages. 3 A flexible approach to working. 4 A commitment to support your health and wellbeing, including a free 6-month UofG Sport membership for all new staff joining the University  https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/staff/healthwellbeing/. We believe that we can only reach our full potential through the talents of all. Equality, diversity and inclusion are at the heart of our values. Applications are particularly welcome from across our communities and in particular people from the Black, Asian and Minority Ethnic (BAME) community, and other protected characteristics who are under-represented within the University. Read more on how the University promotes and embeds all aspects of equality and diversity within our community https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/humanresources/equalitydiversity/. We endorse the principles of Athena Swan https://www.gla.ac.uk/myglasgow/humanresources/equalitydiversity/athenaswan/ and hold bronze, silver and gold awards across the University. We are investing in our organisation, and we will invest in you too. Please visit our website https://www.gla.ac.uk/explore/jobs/ for more information. Apply Now Save this job Student Success Assistant Glasgow Posted 2 months ago Apply Now Share this job linkedin twitter facebook mail whatsapp Sign up for job alerts Sign in or register here to receive emails about new jobs like these You agree to our Terms & conditions Latest Jobs Research Associate in Evidence Synthesis Glasgow GRADE 7, £41,064-£46,049 per annum People and OD (HR) Systems Coordinator Glasgow Grade 6, £33,951 - £37,694 per annum Research Associate - Applied Research & Knowledge Exchange Glasgow Grade 7:  £41,064 - £46,049 per annum Research Associate - Delivering Humanised Places Glasgow Grade 7:  £41,064 - £46,049 per annum Project Manager Glasgow £41,064 pa - £46,049 pa logo The University of Glasgow is a registered Scottishcharity: Registration Number SC004401 X Facebook instagram Legal Cookies Terms of use PeopleXD accessibilty statement Candidate privacy notice Accreditation Quick Links About Contact Us Vacancies Salaries Applicant Guide All Right Reserved 2026 University of Glasgow Recruitment website by"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.gla.ac.uk/job/graduate-teaching-assistant-school-of-psychology-and-neuroscience-5980576": {
      "title": "Graduate Teaching Assistant",
      "description": "Graduate Teaching Assistant - School of · University of Glasgow Vacancies About Us Contact Why UofG Login Register Login Menu Vacancies About Us Why UofG Contact Sign In Home Jobs Graduate Teaching Assistant - School of Psychology & Neuroscience Graduate Teaching Assistant - School of Psychology & Neuroscience Back to jobs Graduate Teaching Assistant - School of Psychology & Neuroscience Job details Posted 29 July 2026 Location Glasgow Job Type GTA and Demonstrator Reference 204593 Expiry 29 Jan 2027 22:59 Job description Applications for this post will only be processed where you are a University of Glasgow student and have been given the vacancy number by your Local School Administrator. Graduate Teaching Assistant – Grade 6 Job Purpose Within a clear and established Psychology teaching programme, contribute to the delivery of an excellent student experience by undertaking a range of teaching, assessment and administrative duties, at undergraduate and/or postgraduate level, to further the understanding of key course topics and assist students in drawing out key learning points from course materials. Expert knowledge of, and the ability to support students with, the programming language R and/or qualitative methodologies (essential). Main Duties and Responsibilities 1.    Contribute to the planning, organisation and delivery of teaching. 2.    Deliver a range of teaching and assessment activities. 3.    Participate in a range of course administration duties, within required timescales, including effective communication of information, marking, assessment and timeous constructive feedback. 4.    Assist with the development of appropriate teaching materials ensuring content and methods of delivery meet determined learning objectives. 5.    Contribute to the effective use of learning technologies to support and enhance course delivery, course organisation, feedback and assessment. This may involve blended and/or online provision including the use of Moodle and other IT materials. 6.    Apply specialist knowledge to teaching that best meet the needs of individuals and groups of learners, ensuring an inclusive and evidence-based approach that promotes student participation and learning outcome attainment. 7.    Participate in the full assessment process using a variety of methods and techniques, including oral assessment, and provide effective, timely and appropriate feedback to students that supports their learning. 8.    Assist with the supervision of student projects, dissertations, and any practical work, advising on skills, methods and techniques to assist the transfer of knowledge, and respond appropriately to the diverse range of learner support/ needs. 9.     Engage in continuing professional development activities as appropriate. 10.   Undertake any other reasonable duties as required by the Head of School. 11.   To contribute to the enhancement of the University’s international profile in line with the University’s Strategic Plan. Knowledge, Qualifications, Skills & Experience Knowledge & Qualifications Essential: A1. Honours degree (SCQF Level 10) relevant to the teaching area (Psychology) or have expertise in a relevant field, together with an understanding of the principles of teaching, learning and assessment. A2. Registered for and working towards the achievement of a PhD qualification in a relevant subject. Skills Essential: C1. Expert knowledge of the subject area (Psychology), including the programming language R and/or qualitative research methodologies. C2. Relevant administrative experience (e.g. student support, course administration etc.) (desired) C3. Excellent communication and presentation skills. C4. Ability to use IT and relevant software packages to support teaching and learning. C5. Proven ability to work independently and as part of a team. C6. Ability to work to deadlines. C7. Commitment to the University’s published values and professional behaviours. Experience Essential: E1. Experience of delivering and supporting undergraduate or postgraduate students either in an online, distance learning or face to face environment (desired). E2. Experience of supervising, mentoring or teaching students in practical and active learning environments(desired). Apply Now Save this job Graduate Teaching Assistant - School of Psychology & Neuroscience Glasgow Posted 3 months ago Apply Now Share this job linkedin twitter facebook mail whatsapp Sign up for job alerts Sign in or register here to receive emails about new jobs like these You agree to our Terms & conditions Latest Jobs Research Associate in Evidence Synthesis Glasgow GRADE 7, £41,064-£46,049 per annum People and OD (HR) Systems Coordinator Glasgow Grade 6, £33,951 - £37,694 per annum Research Associate - Applied Research & Knowledge Exchange Glasgow Grade 7:  £41,064 - £46,049 per annum Research Associate - Delivering Humanised Places Glasgow Grade 7:  £41,064 - £46,049 per annum Project Manager Glasgow £41,064 pa - £46,049 pa logo The University of Glasgow is a registered Scottishcharity: Registration Number SC004401 X Facebook instagram Legal Cookies Terms of use PeopleXD accessibilty statement Candidate privacy notice Accreditation Quick Links About Contact Us Vacancies Salaries Applicant Guide All Right Reserved 2026 University of Glasgow Recruitment website by"
    },
    "https://www.jobs.gla.ac.uk/job/assistant-director-wellbeing-and-student-success-5973298": {
      "title": "Assistant Director",
      "description": "Assistant Director - Wellbeing & Student · University of Glasgow Vacancies About Us Contact Why UofG Login Register Login Menu Vacancies About Us Why UofG Contact Sign In Home Jobs Assistant Director - Wellbeing & Student Success Assistant Director - Wellbeing & Student Success Back to jobs Assistant Director - Wellbeing & Student Success Job details Posted 22 July 2026 Salary Grade 9, £59,966 - £67,468 per annum End date 21 October 2026 Location Glasgow Job Type Management Professional & Administrative Reference 201533 Expiry 21 Oct 2026 23:45 Job description The University of Glasgow is seeking an outstanding leader to join Student & Academic Services as Assistant Director - Wellbeing & Student Success. Reporting to the Director of Student Wellbeing, you will provide strategic and operational leadership for the University's Counselling, Wellbeing and Disability Services, ensuring the delivery of high-quality, evidence-informed support that enhances student success, inclusion, accessibility, retention and wellbeing. As a senior leader within Student & Academic Services, you will play a key role in shaping strategy and driving continuous improvement across student support services. You will lead multidisciplinary teams, develop innovative approaches to student wellbeing and inclusion, and ensure services remain responsive to the evolving needs of students and the wider higher education sector. Working collaboratively with senior colleagues across the University and with external partners, you will lead on strategic planning, service development, change management and operational excellence. You will also oversee compliance with relevant legislation, regulatory requirements and sector best practice relating to disability, equality, and student support. We are seeking a highly experienced leader with substantial expertise in mental health, wellbeing, disability, inclusion or student support services. You will have a proven track record of leading complex services, developing and implementing strategy, managing change, and delivering measurable service improvements within a large and complex organisation. You will possess exceptional leadership, communication and stakeholder management skills, with the ability to influence at all levels, build effective partnerships and inspire high-performing teams. Experience of financial management, governance and quality assurance is essential. This is a unique opportunity to make a lasting impact on the student experience and help shape the future of wellbeing, inclusion and accessibility at our institution. Job Purpose To play a lead role in the successful implementation and delivery of the Student & Academic Services vision, alongside the key strategies that support student success. Annual objectives will fully achieve key University goals and deliver improvements evidenced by internal and external measures. This will extend to making significant contributions to shaping the long-term strategic direction of the University, in partnership with senior academic and professional leaders. ​ The role reports to the Director of Student Wellbeing and has responsibility to the Executive Director of Student and Academic Services to provide significant leadership to determine and deliver the long-term structure of professional services within S&AS, aligned and contributing to university-level changes as appropriate. ​ The postholder will have strategic and operational responsibility for the University's Counselling, Wellbeing and Disability Services, ensuring the delivery of high-quality, evidence-informed services that support student success, inclusion, retention, safety and wellbeing. The post holder will bring significant specialist knowledge and leadership expertise in student mental health, wellbeing, disability inclusion, accessibility, and the wider regulatory and strategic context affecting these areas within higher education. Main Duties and Responsibilities 1.  Working as part of the S&AS Leadership Group, the postholder will make significant contributions to the development and delivery of Directorate priorities and strategies in line with the University's strategic objectives, evidenced by KPI's and targets such as student satisfaction, continuation, student success and return on investment. The role will lead the strategic planning process for Mental Health & Wellbeing and Disability Services and will assume lead responsibility for implementation of significant work streams, partnering on matters across Schools, Colleges and University Services. 2.  Manage and develop the professional, administrative, operational and technical support functions and structures within the areas of responsibility to secure effective delivery of priorities and KPI targets. This includes leadership of multidisciplinary specialist teams delivering mental health, wellbeing, disability and inclusion services. The role will provide significant leadership in resource planning to determine and deliver the long-term structure of professional services within S&AS, aligned to and contributing to University-level changes as appropriate. 3.  Lead horizon scanning for the areas of responsibility and as part of S&AS to future-proof performance sustainability within an increasingly competitive HE environment. This will include monitoring emerging trends, legislation, policy developments and sector expectations relating to student mental health, disability support, accessibility, safeguarding, risk management and student wellbeing. The role will involve regular reporting on potential changes to the operating model and strategic positioning of the areas of responsibility for S&AS Executive consideration. 4.  Lead contributor to the financial planning process for the areas of responsibility and work with the Director of Student Wellbeing to manage and lead financial strategy and operational delivery. 5.  Participate in regular reviews including competitor benchmarking, developing plans to improve performance in key areas of focus, including student experience and student success. This role will also provide leadership to ensure continuous improvement within assigned business areas. 6.  The postholder will work with senior colleagues to develop and manage external partnerships and networks to enhance the University's reputation at local, national and international levels. This will include engagement with relevant professional bodies, NHS partners, third-sector organisations, disability networks and sector agencies involved in advancing student wellbeing and inclusion. 7.  Make a senior contribution to the successful delivery of key change management programmes across the University, driving measurable improvement in key performance areas. This role will also lead assigned change programmes, driving efficiency gains and improving service provision to students. 8.  Ensure effective compliance, as required, with the University's governance framework, sourcing appropriate support from specialist and professional colleagues across the institution. This will include ensuring best practice in relation to all relevant legal, regulatory and sector requirements relating to disability, equality and student support. 9.  Contribute to University-wide process improvement programmes, driving efficiency gains and improving service provision to students and staff, providing SAS leadership aligned with University expectations. The role will be assigned University-wide responsibilities to lead and significantly contribute to improvement programmes. 10.  In partnership with senior colleagues, develop a robust framework to support compliance with relevant accreditation bodies, regulatory requirements and quality assurance processes. This will include external engagement with relevant bodies influencing the future strategy and focus of student wellbeing, mental health and disability provision. 11.  Undertake other duties commensurate with the role as assigned by the Director of Student Wellbeing. Knowledge, Qualifications, Skills & Experience Knowledge/Qualifications Essential: A1 Ability to demonstrate the competencies required to undertake the duties associated with this level of post, having acquired the necessary professional knowledge and management skills through experience in a similar role or a number of different roles. A2 Scottish Credit and Qualification Framework level 9, 10 or 11 (Honours Degree, Postgraduate Qualification) or equivalent, including being professionally qualified in a relevant discipline, with a broad range of professional experience in a similar management role. A3 Significant understanding of the challenges affecting student mental health and wellbeing within higher education, and of evidence-informed approaches to prevention, early intervention and support. A4 Significant knowledge of disability legislation, inclusive practice, reasonable adjustments and the strategic delivery of disability services in a complex organisational environment. A5 Knowledge of governance and business processes in a large complex organisation. A6 Broad knowledge of financial management within a budgetary control framework and an understanding of financial planning and business analytics. Desirable: B1 A relevant professional qualification in mental health, wellbeing, disability, inclusion, student support or a related field. Skills Essential: C1 Excellent communication and interpersonal skills, particularly influencing, negotiation and diplomacy. C2 Highly effective strategic leadership skills as a business partner. C3 Ability to demonstrate resilience and able to maintain momentum in the face of challenges and setbacks. C4 Excellent customer service, stakeholder management and business partnering skills. C5 Proven ability to develop, deliver and articulate a clear vision and to develop operational plans to support strategic initiatives. C6 Excellent ability to empower and motivate others to deliver a high-performance culture in a participative manner. Experience E1 Extensive relevant experience in leading service delivery in areas of mental health, wellbeing, disability, inclusion and student support, including service development, strategy, risk management and quality assurance. preferably in a Higher Education setting. E2 Demonstrable experience of leading multidisciplinary specialist teams with experience of developing strategy and influencing institutional policy and practice to improve inclusion, wellbeing, accessibility and student experience. E3 Demonstrable and sustained track record of leading successful change projects across a complex organisation, with evidence of transformational impact and business process improvement. E4 Extensive and recent administrative management or strategic leadership experience in a large complex organisation, working with senior colleagues and stakeholders in the development and implementation of strategic objectives, strategies and major projects. Terms and Conditions Salary will be Grade 9, £59,966 - £67,468 per annum. This post is full time (35 hours per week) and open ended (permanent).  Relocation assistance will be provided where appropriate. The University of Glasgow has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK.  If you require a Skilled Worker visa to work in the UK, you will be required to meet the eligibility requirements of the visa route to be assigned a Certificate of Sponsorship. Please note that this post may be eligible to be sponsored under the Skilled Worker visa route if tradeable points can be used under the Skilled Worker visa rules. For more information please visit: https://www.gov.uk/skilled-worker-visa. As a valued member of our team, you can expect: 1 A warm welcoming and engaging organisational culture, where your talents are developed and nurtured, and success is celebrated and shared. 2 An excellent employment package with generous terms and conditions including 41 days of leave for full time staff, pension , benefits and discount packages. 3 A flexible approach to working. 4 A commitment to support your health and wellbeing, including a free 6-month UofG Sport membership for all new staff joining the University We believe that we can only reach our full potential through the talents of all. Equality, diversity and inclusion are at the heart of our values. Applications are particularly welcome from across our communities and in particular people from the Black, Asian and Minority Ethnic (BAME) community, and other protected characteristics who are under-represented within the University. Read more on how the University promotes and embeds all aspects of equality and diversity within our community here . We endorse the principles of Athena Swan and hold bronze, silver and gold awards across the University. We are investing in our organisation, and we will invest in you too. Please visit our website https://www.gla.ac.uk/explore/jobs/ for more information. Closing Date:  21st October 2026 at 23:45 Provisional Interview Date: Friday 30th October 2026. Apply Now Save this job Assistant Director - Wellbeing & Student Success Glasgow Posted 3 months ago Apply Now Share this job linkedin twitter facebook mail whatsapp Sign up for job alerts Sign in or register here to receive emails about new jobs like these You agree to our Terms & conditions Latest Jobs Research Associate in Evidence Synthesis Glasgow GRADE 7, £41,064-£46,049 per annum People and OD (HR) Systems Coordinator Glasgow Grade 6, £33,951 - £37,694 per annum Research Associate - Applied Research & Knowledge Exchange Glasgow Grade 7:  £41,064 - £46,049 per annum Research Associate - Delivering Humanised Places Glasgow Grade 7:  £41,064 - £46,049 per annum Project Manager Glasgow £41,064 pa - £46,049 pa logo The University of Glasgow is a registered Scottishcharity: Registration Number SC004401 X Facebook instagram Legal Cookies Terms of use PeopleXD accessibilty statement Candidate privacy notice Accreditation Quick Links About Contact Us Vacancies Salaries Applicant Guide All Right Reserved 2026 University of Glasgow Recruitment website by"
